2. DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THREE TYPES OF DI
==============================================================================

A. CONSTRUCTOR INJECTION - RECOMMENDED

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    private final PaymentRepository repository;

    public PaymentService(PaymentRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Advantages:

• Dependency is mandatory
• Supports final fields
• Easy unit testing
• Object cannot be created in an incomplete state

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

B. SETTER INJECTION

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    private PaymentRepository repository;

    @Autowired
    public void setRepository(PaymentRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Use:

Optional or replaceable dependency.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

C. FIELD INJECTION

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    @Autowired
    private PaymentRepository repository;
}

Disadvantages:

• Harder to unit test
• Dependency is hidden
• Cannot use final
• Not recommended for new code

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

BEST ANSWER:

"Constructor injection is preferred for mandatory dependencies, setter
injection is suitable for optional dependencies, and field injection is
concise but harder to test and maintain."

