9. WHAT DOES MAX() DO? WHAT ABOUT NULL?
==============================================================================

MAX() is an aggregate function that returns the maximum non-null value.

Query:

SELECT MAX(salary)
FROM employee;

Given:

10000
20000
NULL
15000

Result:

20000

Behavior:

• MAX ignores NULL values.
• If every salary is NULL, MAX returns NULL.
• If no rows qualify, MAX also returns NULL.

Optional default value:

SELECT COALESCE(MAX(salary), 0)
FROM employee;

Result when no non-null salary exists:

0

