19. DEFAULT SPRING BEAN SCOPE
==============================================================================

The default Spring bean scope is:

singleton

Example:

@Service
public class PaymentService {
}

Spring creates one shared PaymentService bean instance per Spring IoC
container. 【2-090452】

Important:

Spring singleton means one instance per Spring container, not necessarily one
instance across every application, JVM, or Kubernetes Pod.

==============================================================================
20. ADVANTAGE OF SINGLETON SCOPE
==============================================================================

Advantages:

• Less object creation
• Lower memory overhead
• Bean can be reused
• Suitable for stateless services
• Centralized lifecycle management

Important:

Singleton beans should generally avoid mutable shared request-specific data,
because multiple threads may use the same bean.

Good:

public Payment process(Payment payment) {
    return repository.save(payment);
}

Risky:

private String currentUser;

The second field could be overwritten by concurrent requests.

==============================================================================
21. CAN WE CHANGE THE BEAN SCOPE?
==============================================================================

Yes.

Example:

@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class ReportBuilder {
}

Other common scopes include:

• singleton
• prototype
• request
• session
• application
• websocket

Request and session scopes require a web-aware application context.
